      {
        "id": "u3",
        "kind": "CONCEPT",
        "label": "Historical origin and meaning of the name Alkaline Earth",
        "attributes": {
          "depth": "explanation"
        },
        "subtopic_ids": [
          "s2"
        ],
        "rationale": "Explains why the elements are designated 'alkaline' (forming basic solutions with water) and 'earth' (alchemical terminology for heat-resistant, insoluble oxides).",
        "review_flags": [],
        "contextualization": {
          "value": "none",
          "evidence": []
        },
        "passages": [
          {
            "id": "p15",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "heading"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "### 2. Why are they called \"Alkaline Earth\"?",
            "start": 774,
            "end": 818
          },
          {
            "id": "p16",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "The name sounds fancy, but it comes from two simple historical ideas:",
            "start": 819,
            "end": 888
          },
          {
            "id": "p17",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "explanation"
            },
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "1.  **Alkaline:** When these metals react with water, they form basic (alkaline) solutions—meaning they have a high pH (the opposite of acidic).",
            "start": 889,
            "end": 1033
          },
          {
            "id": "p18",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "2.  **Earth:** Early scientists and alchemists used the word \"earths\" to describe substances that were insoluble in water and didn't melt easily under fire (specifically, their oxides). ",
            "start": 1034,
            "end": 1220
          },
          {
            "id": "p19",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Put them together, and you get *Alkaline Earth Metals*!",
            "start": 1222,
            "end": 1277
          },
          {
            "id": "p20",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "separator"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "---",
            "start": 1279,
            "end": 1282
          }
        ],
        "accuracy": {
          "verdict": "accurate",
          "reason": "Correctly explains the historical derivation of both 'alkaline' and 'earth'.",
          "errors": []
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "u4",
        "kind": "CONCEPT",
        "label": "Valence electrons, cation formation, and high chemical reactivity",
        "attributes": {
          "depth": "explanation"
        },
        "subtopic_ids": [
          "s3"
        ],
        "rationale": "Explains how having two valence electrons leads to the loss of two electrons according to the octet rule, forming +2 cations and causing high elemental reactivity.",
        "review_flags": [],
        "contextualization": {
          "value": "none",
          "evidence": []
        },
        "passages": [
          {
            "id": "p21",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "heading"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "### 3. The Chemistry Secret: The \"Rule of Two\"",
            "start": 1284,
            "end": 1330
          },
          {
            "id": "p22",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "In high school chemistry, the most important rule to remember is: **Valence electrons determine behavior.**",
            "start": 1331,
            "end": 1438
          },
          {
            "id": "p23",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "*   Every single element in Group 2 has **2 valence electrons** (outermost electrons).",
            "start": 1440,
            "end": 1526
          },
          {
            "id": "p24",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "*   All atoms want a stable outer shell of 8 electrons (the Octet Rule).",
            "start": 1527,
            "end": 1599
          },
          {
            "id": "p25",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "explanation"
            },
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "*   For these metals, it is *much easier* to kick out those 2 extra electrons than to hunt for 6 more.",
            "start": 1600,
            "end": 1702
          },
          {
            "id": "p26",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "explanation"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Because they lose 2 negatively charged electrons, they always form **$+2$ ions** (written as $\\text{Mg}^{2+}$, $\\text{Ca}^{2+}$, etc.). ",
            "start": 1704,
            "end": 1840
          },
          {
            "id": "p27",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "heading"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "**Reactivity:** ",
            "start": 1842,
            "end": 1858
          },
          {
            "id": "p28",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "explanation"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Because they are eager to lose those 2 electrons, they are **very reactive**. You will never find pure, raw calcium or magnesium just lying around in nature; they are always bonded to other elements (like oxygen, chlorine, or carbon).",
            "start": 1859,
            "end": 2093
          },
          {
            "id": "p29",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "separator"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "---",
            "start": 2095,
            "end": 2098
          }
        ],
        "accuracy": {
          "verdict": "accurate",
          "reason": "The explanation of valence electrons, octet stability, +2 charge formulation, and natural bonded states is standard and scientifically accurate.",
          "errors": []
        }
      },
